{{result}}

nodes.prompt:
A coding job is about to plan this change in the trusted folder {{folder}}: {{goal}}

Find the code the change belongs in, and read it. Also find out whether the project already does part of this (a class, file, setting or mechanism for the same thing, even under another name): read it, and say in the answer what exists and whether the change should extend it. The request is in the user's words, not the code's: start with ONE findParts call that holds every word the request could use (the UI words and the likely code names together, e.g. "conversation row menu button folder sidebar"): it ranks the parts of the project by how many of the terms they use and answers path · section id · lines. Never search one term per call. where shows one exact name's definitions and uses; searchFiles is for a text or pattern. Then read the parts that matter with readSection, each whole: the code that does the thing now, and, when the request says "like X" or "the same as X", the code of X too. Compare them: what exactly differs. outline lists the parts of a file; findFiles and listFiles find files. Read only what the request names (the file, the method) and what that code needs to be changed correctly: not other files or siblings for context. If other places have the same problem, say so in your answer; they are not part of this change. Read about ten tool calls at most; never repeat a call.

Then answer in at most four lines: the file and section id where the change belongs, what must change there, and what the project already has for it (or "nothing existing"). If the request does not say what to change, say in two lines what is unclear. No lists of what you saw: the parts you read whole go to the plan as they are.

You did a coding job in the trusted folder {{folder}}: {{task}}

Your report was:
{{result}}

The build check that runs after the work FAILED. Its output:
{{check}}

Find what is wrong in the code and fix it with the tools (outline and readSection show the code, editSection and patchFile change it; never write files with commands). Change only what the messages point at. Environment: {{environment}}

Then answer with a report of at most 3 short lines: what was wrong and what you changed.
